lete (AIN), but the International Olympic Committee did not declare him neutral and he was not invited to the Games. The World champions from 2022 and 2023 were " [:a {:href "/athletes/145337"} "Carlos Sansores"] " from Mexico and " [:a {:href "/athletes/133231"} "Cheick Sallah Cissé"] " from the Ivory Coast. The latter won the welterweight class at the 2016 Olympics. Neither of the two former World champions, however, managed to reach the final."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The gold medal match was between two athletes born in 2003. Iran’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/155014"} "Arian Salimi"] " won gold by beating Britain’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/155007"} "Caden Cunningham"] ". Cuba’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/133385"} "Rafael Alba"] " and Cheick Sallah Cissé claimed bronze. In a stunning display of skill and determination, the 21-year-old Cunningham secured a place in the final by toppling three seasoned Olympic medalists in a row. Cunningham began by overcoming Niger’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/135337"} "Razak Alfaga"] ", the Rio 2016 silver medalist, in the round of 16. Then, in the quarter-final, he dispatched Tokyo bronze medalist Rafael Alba, and in the semi Cunningham beat Cissé, known as the “Man of Rio” for securing Ivory Coast’s first-ever Olympic gold medal."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Bib/Seed"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "810"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/155014"} "Arian Salimi"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/IRI"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/IRI.png"}] "IRI"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "804"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/155007"} "Caden Cunningham"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-righ